Unmap the shm buffers a destroyed window leaves behind

create_buffer() maps stride*height bytes and hands the pointer to
cairo_image_surface_create_for_data(). Cairo draws into that memory without
owning it, and the wl_buffer only references it, so when destroy_buffer()
destroys both and memsets the struct, the mapping is left with nothing
pointing at it. struct buffer had nowhere to record the pointer or the length
either, which is why the munmap could not simply be added here: this stores
both, at the first point where they are known.

Nothing noticed upstream because bemenu is a one-shot program. It builds one
window, draws it, and exits, so a mapping leaked at teardown is one the kernel
reclaims a moment later regardless. beamenu runs as a resident daemon and
builds a fresh menu per launcher invocation, which turns the same bug into one
panel's worth of RSS per keypress: measured at a flat 3444 kB per show over
twelve consecutive show/dismiss cycles on a 1920x1080 output, rising without
bound for as long as the session lasts.

next_buffer() also destroys and recreates a buffer whenever the panel's size
changes, so this leaked on output and scale changes even in the one-shot case.

diff --git a/lib/renderers/wayland/wayland.h b/lib/renderers/wayland/wayland.h
index ef3859e..8744990 100644
--- a/lib/renderers/wayland/wayland.h
+++ b/lib/renderers/wayland/wayland.h
@@ -110,6 +110,12 @@ struct input {
 struct buffer {
     struct cairo cairo;
     struct wl_buffer *buffer;
+    /* The shm mapping cairo's image surface draws into. Cairo does not own it
+     * and the wl_buffer only references it, so destroy_buffer is the only
+     * place that can hand it back — and the only place both the pointer and
+     * the length are still known. */
+    void *data;
+    size_t size;
     uint32_t width, height;
     bool busy;
 };
diff --git a/lib/renderers/wayland/window.c b/lib/renderers/wayland/window.c
index 043deab..4739462 100644
--- a/lib/renderers/wayland/window.c
+++ b/lib/renderers/wayland/window.c
@@ -104,6 +104,10 @@ destroy_buffer(struct buffer *buffer)
     if (buffer->buffer)
         wl_buffer_destroy(buffer->buffer);
     bm_cairo_destroy(&buffer->cairo);
+    /* After bm_cairo_destroy, so nothing can still be flushing into the
+     * mapping as it goes away. */
+    if (buffer->data)
+        munmap(buffer->data, buffer->size);
     memset(buffer, 0, sizeof(struct buffer));
 }
 
@@ -127,6 +131,11 @@ create_buffer(struct wl_shm *shm, struct buffer *buffer, int32_t width, int32_t
         return false;
     }
 
+    /* Recorded before the first failure path that runs destroy_buffer, so an
+     * aborted create unmaps what it already mapped. */
+    buffer->data = data;
+    buffer->size = size;
+
     if (!(pool = wl_shm_create_pool(shm, fd, size))) {
         close(fd);
         return false;
